Ladybird reflects CSS animation keyframes from their declaration groups,
with computed values, per-frame easing and composite operations, and
underlying values for missing endpoints. It applies pending CSS changes
and computes values without publishing an effect stack or modifying its
animation overlays. CSS transitions expose their stored endpoint
values. Script-supplied keyframes retain their specified values and take
precedence over subsequent changes to matching CSS keyframe rules.

Blink’s KeyframeEffect::getKeyframes flushes pending animation updates.
CssKeyframeEffectModel::GetComputedKeyframes resolves CSS declarations
and supplies implicit endpoint values from the underlying style. Its
TransitionKeyframe::AddKeyframePropertiesToV8Object serializes stored
transition values. Ladybird follows this computed-value approach.

Gecko’s KeyframeEffect::GetKeyframes serializes declarations through
Servo_DeclarationBlock_SerializeOneValue, which uses the target’s style
to substitute variables but otherwise preserves specified CSS values.
CSSAnimationKeyframeEffect::GetComputedKeyframes fills missing endpoints
from base values. WebKit’s KeyframeEffect::getKeyframes distinguishes
parsed script frames from style-originated blending keyframes, fills
implicit endpoints, and also preserves specified text where possible.
Ladybird matches Blink when those engines retain relative CSS lengths.

Ladybird keeps explicitly declared endpoint frames before synthesized
frames at the same offset, matching Chrome and the imported WPT. It also
uses replacement endpoints for underlying values, including on additive
effects, and animation-wide easing for substitution-dependent easing.
The current CSS Animations draft instead prepends the initial frame and
uses the default composite and computed keyframe easing. The reflection
code marks these interoperability choices with AD-HOC comments.

The retained underlying style has already coordinated background lists
with background-image. Reflection preserves all explicitly declared
keyframe layers, but can’t recover discarded underlying layers. The
computed-value serializer also retains an explicit zero second argument
in translate(), and converts filter percentages to equivalent numbers.
